// `private` — the first visibility Burxt has ever had.
//
// The class is the SCOPE: a private field or method is reachable only from that class's own
// methods. Before v0.0.150 there was no privacy at all — a file could reach into a
// transitively-imported file's helpers and read any type's fields directly, bypassing whatever
// method it provided.
//
// FILE-level privacy is deliberately absent, and the reason is structural rather than a
// shortfall: `use` is a text pre-pass that concatenates files, so by the time anything is
// checked there are no files, only one long program. A class needs no such knowledge, because
// it is its own boundary. That is why `class` had to land before `private` could.
//
// ---- It needed a constructor, which stage-1 could not hold (until v0.0.165) ----------
//
// This lived in tests/stage0-only/ because it needs a CONSTRUCTOR. Stage-0 stores an associated
// function under the qualified name `Account.open`; stage-1 names every declaration by its SPAN
// in the source, and `Account.open` is not contiguous — the class name and the function name are
// far apart — so there appeared to be nothing to point at, and the plan of record was to give
// stage-1 synthesized names.
//
// That turned out to be the wrong fix for the wrong problem. Stage-1 already keys a METHOD by
// two spans, a receiver and a name, because that is how `Account` + `shown` is looked up. A
// qualified name IS a name in two parts, so an associated function is a method with bit 32 set
// and no receiver argument, and the lookup that already existed finds it. No name is built
// anywhere, in either compiler.
//
// What still wants synthesized names is narrower than it looked: M13's return bracket, where the
// word `result` is never written down and so has no span in any program that uses it.
class Account {
    owner: String,
    private balance: Decimal<2>,

    // Its own methods reach its own privates — both the field and the helper.
    function (self) statement() -> String {
        return self.owner + " has " + to_string(self.spendable());
    }

    // An implementation detail, and now sayable as one.
    private function (self) spendable() -> Decimal<2> {
        return self.balance;
    }

    // An ASSOCIATED function — no `self`, because it makes one. Called `Account.open(...)`,
    // and it is the only way to build an Account, because nothing outside this class may name
    // `balance` in a literal. That is what makes the `requires` below a real guarantee rather
    // than a suggestion.
    function open(owner: String, opening: Decimal<2>) -> Account
            requires opening >= 0.00
            {
        return Account { owner: owner, balance: opening };
    }
}

// A method written OUTSIDE the class body is still a method OF the class, so it reaches the
// privates too. The boundary is the type, not the block — there is no file boundary to appeal
// to, and inventing one here would be a rule with no mechanism behind it.
function (self: Account) shouted() -> String {
    return "BALANCE " + to_string(self.balance);
}

// Everything public is reachable as before.
class Item {
    sku: String,
    price: Decimal<2>,
    function (self) label() -> String { return self.sku; }
}

// A literal may set a private field only INSIDE the class, so this needs the constructor —
// which is the whole point. Until v0.0.151 construction was exempt, because with no
// constructors the rule would have made such a class impossible to build at all, and
// `private` therefore could not defend an invariant.
let a: Account = Account.open("ada", 10.00);
print(a.statement());
print(a.shouted());

let rice: Item = Item { sku: "RICE", price: 52.75 };
print(rice.label());
print(rice.price);
